Erdos339: both original density questions for restricted sumsets

Target: https://www.erdosproblems.com/339
Justin Sun Prize catalog: JSP-000281.
The full project includes the asymptotic-basis/lower-density theorem and the
positive-upper-density theorem, for every set of natural numbers and order r.

Mathematics: Norbert Hegyvári, François Hennecart, Alain Plagne,
J. Reine Angew. Math. 560 (2003), 199-220.
Upstream finite combinatorial and lower-density formalization:
plby/lean-proofs, formal authors Codex and GPT-5.6 Sol, commit
8822f7ddef30fadbd92e1c6ab4ed897af356af5e.
https://github.com/plby/lean-proofs/blob/8822f7ddef30fadbd92e1c6ab4ed897af356af5e/src/latest/ErdosProblems/Erdos339.lean
The upper-density extension and reproducible full-problem integration were
developed with an AI coding assistant for the submitting GitHub account.
Only this incremental formalization contribution is offered for evaluation.
No new mathematical discovery, sole authorship of reused proofs, established
priority, independently certified result, or award decision is claimed.

Upstream Erdos339.lean has no stated redistribution license.
Finite.lean and LowerDensity.lean are generated locally by bootstrap.py from
that immutable source, with a checked SHA-256, and excluded from Git.
This repository does not license or redistribute those generated files.
The unlicensed upstream dependency remains a review/redistribution limitation.
One disclosed compatibility change is made to the finite proof; the lower
endpoint's proof body is retained. Basis.lean supplies only the conventional
cofinite-sumset definition and its elementary atTop equivalence.
Util/Density.lean retains its explicit Formal Conjectures Authors copyright
and Apache-2.0 notice; LICENSE-APACHE-2.0 applies to that utility, not to the
unlicensed dependency.

Reproduce with Python 3.10+ and elan/Lean on PATH:
  python3 bootstrap.py
  lake exe cache get
  lake build
  lake env lean Audit.lean
Lean 4.32.1 and all Mathlib dependencies are pinned in the project files.
GitHub Actions on complete-original-problem executes the same build and
checks all four target axiom reports. See README.md for evidence limitations.
